The traffic in the Los Angeles region is known as some of the worst in the world. If you live in Santa Ana, you know how important it is to be behind the wheel of a car you can trust - especially in dangerous jams. The last thing you need to add to an already messy equation is a faulty vehicle.

Lemon cars are all too common throughout California. Substandard manufacturing leads to a vehicle that’s unreliable and always experiencing issues. Even if you purchase from a reliable seller, there’s always the chance that you could wind up seated in a sour vehicle.

That’s why “lemon law” exists. The Song Beverly Consumer Warranty Act (California lemon law) holds manufacturers accountable for defective, irreparable vehicles. It protects Santa Ana drivers who get saddled with a car that never should have been sold. At CLINE APC, our legal team is committed to helping local consumers understand their rights under California lemon law. An experienced Santa Ana Lemon Law Attorney can evaluate a claim, explain available legal options, and guide drivers through the process of seeking compensation, a vehicle replacement, or a refund for a qualifying lemon vehicle.

All of our Santa Ana [lemon law attorneys](/) at CLINE APC are happy to educate locals on their rights. We’ll show you how to seek fair compensation for your lemon car.

These factors indicate whether manufacturer mistakes entitle you to compensation under California lemon law:

- The vehicle manufacturer has made at least two attempts to repair a warranty-covered defect, or

- The vehicle has been out of service for more than 30 days while being repaired for warranty-covered problems; and

- The vehicle has several warranty-covered problems, which were not caused by abuse of the vehicle and that impair its use, value, or safety.

It’s important to note that there is a time limit for seeking compensation under California lemon law - even with the aid of a lemon lawyer in Santa Ana. Your lemon car’s defect must occur within the defined terms of the manufacturer’s or dealer’s warranty.

Despite the common misunderstanding, your vehicle’s issue does not need to be addressed within the first 18 months after purchase or before the odometer reaches 18,000 miles. The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty.

When your vehicle meets California lemon law requirements, you’re entitled to:

Full coverage of your Santa Ana lemon law lawyer and court costs A full refund of the vehicle’s original acquisition price and any incidental costs A replacement vehicle, if necessary

